{
  "title": "Prime Intellect Blogpost",
  "documentId": "1HqP4nLDMz-PcZbxPhZgdVJgVZOjKsOAr9IqzIRUVE9k",
  "revisionId": "ANLCKQlTgYhE15YrckljvUhlW6D-775jr0CukRACV7vxsUk6EnFxrLuXmD_Hl8fQ-bSQ2mtOI48VLD6o3vPBMnOudYesdftKSOtHpEYHOws",
  "retrievedOn": "2026-09-13",
  "paragraphs": [
    {
      "elements": [
        {
          "startIndex": 1,
          "endIndex": 98,
          "textRun": {
            "content": "Is Bigger Always Better? Diagnosing How to Improve Vision-Language Models (VLMs) with WorldBench\n",
            "textStyle": {}
          }
        }
      ],
      "paragraphStyle": {
        "headingId": "h.xqahz0coesa4",
        "namedStyleType": "TITLE",
        "direction": "LEFT_TO_RIGHT"
      }
    },
    {
      "elements": [
        {
          "startIndex": 98,
          "endIndex": 99,
          "textRun": {
            "content": "\n",
            "textStyle": {}
          }
        }
      ],
      "paragraphStyle": {
        "namedStyleType": "NORMAL_TEXT",
        "direction": "LEFT_TO_RIGHT"
      }
    },
    {
      "elements": [
        {
          "startIndex": 99,
          "endIndex": 407,
          "textRun": {
            "content": "When a VLM fails at a given task, it’s often instinctual to reach for a larger model. However, while certain failures can stem from missing knowledge or reasoning capacity, others can arise because of the model’s inability to inspect an image at inference time, which doesn’t necessarily change with scale. \n",
            "textStyle": {}
          }
        }
      ],
      "paragraphStyle": {
        "namedStyleType": "NORMAL_TEXT",
        "direction": "LEFT_TO_RIGHT"
      }
    },
    {
      "elements": [
        {
          "startIndex": 407,
          "endIndex": 408,
          "textRun": {
            "content": "\n",
            "textStyle": {}
          }
        }
      ],
      "paragraphStyle": {
        "namedStyleType": "NORMAL_TEXT",
        "direction": "LEFT_TO_RIGHT"
      }
    },
    {
      "elements": [
        {
          "startIndex": 408,
          "endIndex": 484,
          "textRun": {
            "content": "These failure modes suggest two distinct paths to improvement. The first is ",
            "textStyle": {}
          }
        },
        {
          "startIndex": 484,
          "endIndex": 491,
          "textRun": {
            "content": "scaling",
            "textStyle": {
              "bold": true
            }
          }
        },
        {
          "startIndex": 491,
          "endIndex": 605,
          "textRun": {
            "content": ", which can compound the knowledge “stored” by any given model and increase its capacity to reason. The second is ",
            "textStyle": {}
          }
        },
        {
          "startIndex": 605,
          "endIndex": 616,
          "textRun": {
            "content": "scaffolding",
            "textStyle": {
              "bold": true
            }
          }
        },
        {
          "startIndex": 616,
          "endIndex": 727,
          "textRun": {
            "content": ": giving the model a harness to let it programmatically zoom, enhance, and measure an image before answering. \n",
            "textStyle": {}
          }
        }
      ],
      "paragraphStyle": {
        "namedStyleType": "NORMAL_TEXT",
        "direction": "LEFT_TO_RIGHT"
      }
    },
    {
      "elements": [
        {
          "startIndex": 727,
          "endIndex": 728,
          "textRun": {
            "content": "\n",
            "textStyle": {}
          }
        }
      ],
      "paragraphStyle": {
        "namedStyleType": "NORMAL_TEXT",
        "direction": "LEFT_TO_RIGHT"
      }
    },
    {
      "elements": [
        {
          "startIndex": 728,
          "endIndex": 767,
          "textRun": {
            "content": "To investigate these mechanisms, I use ",
            "textStyle": {}
          }
        },
        {
          "startIndex": 767,
          "endIndex": 777,
          "textRun": {
            "content": "WorldBench",
            "textStyle": {
              "bold": true,
              "underline": true,
              "foregroundColor": {
                "color": {
                  "rgbColor": {
                    "red": 0.06666667,
                    "green": 0.33333334,
                    "blue": 0.8
                  }
                }
              },
              "link": {
                "url": "https://worldbench-vl.github.io/"
              }
            }
          }
        },
        {
          "startIndex": 777,
          "endIndex": 823,
          "textRun": {
            "content": ", now available as a Verifiers v1 environment ",
            "textStyle": {}
          }
        },
        {
          "startIndex": 823,
          "endIndex": 834,
          "textRun": {
            "content": "(link here)",
            "textStyle": {
              "italic": true
            }
          }
        },
        {
          "startIndex": 834,
          "endIndex": 1030,
          "textRun": {
            "content": " on the Prime Intellect platform. WorldBench is a 2,000-question VQA benchmark centered on visual diversity, with questions spanning multiple domains and requiring both knowledge and perception. \n",
            "textStyle": {}
          }
        }
      ],
      "paragraphStyle": {
        "namedStyleType": "NORMAL_TEXT",
        "direction": "LEFT_TO_RIGHT"
      }
    },
    {
      "elements": [
        {
          "startIndex": 1030,
          "endIndex": 1031,
          "textRun": {
            "content": "\n",
            "textStyle": {}
          }
        }
      ],
      "paragraphStyle": {
        "namedStyleType": "NORMAL_TEXT",
        "direction": "LEFT_TO_RIGHT"
      }
    },
    {
      "elements": [
        {
          "startIndex": 1031,
          "endIndex": 1229,
          "textRun": {
            "content": "I evaluate each problem with eight rollouts and report both Pass@1 and Pass@8. These experiments were done across four model sizes: Qwen-3.5 4B, Qwen-3.5 9B, Qwen-3.6 27B, and Qwen 3.5 397B (A17B).\n",
            "textStyle": {}
          }
        }
      ],
      "paragraphStyle": {
        "namedStyleType": "NORMAL_TEXT",
        "direction": "LEFT_TO_RIGHT"
      }
    },
    {
      "elements": [
        {
          "startIndex": 1229,
          "endIndex": 1230,
          "textRun": {
            "content": "\n",
            "textStyle": {}
          }
        }
      ],
      "paragraphStyle": {
        "namedStyleType": "NORMAL_TEXT",
        "direction": "LEFT_TO_RIGHT"
      }
    },
    {
      "elements": [
        {
          "startIndex": 1230,
          "endIndex": 1257,
          "textRun": {
            "content": "What Improves With Scale? \n",
            "textStyle": {}
          }
        }
      ],
      "paragraphStyle": {
        "headingId": "h.hfh5d2apptav",
        "namedStyleType": "SUBTITLE",
        "direction": "LEFT_TO_RIGHT"
      }
    },
    {
      "elements": [
        {
          "startIndex": 1257,
          "endIndex": 1403,
          "textRun": {
            "content": "Across all model sizes, scaling consistently improves Pass@1, while Pass@8 remains relatively flat. This suggests that scaling often improves the ",
            "textStyle": {}
          }
        },
        {
          "startIndex": 1403,
          "endIndex": 1414,
          "textRun": {
            "content": "reliability",
            "textStyle": {
              "bold": true
            }
          }
        },
        {
          "startIndex": 1414,
          "endIndex": 1482,
          "textRun": {
            "content": " of a model discovering the right solution, while not expanding the ",
            "textStyle": {}
          }
        },
        {
          "startIndex": 1482,
          "endIndex": 1490,
          "textRun": {
            "content": "coverage",
            "textStyle": {
              "bold": true
            }
          }
        },
        {
          "startIndex": 1490,
          "endIndex": 1530,
          "textRun": {
            "content": " of the solutions the model can solve. \n",
            "textStyle": {}
          }
        }
      ],
      "paragraphStyle": {
        "namedStyleType": "NORMAL_TEXT",
        "direction": "LEFT_TO_RIGHT"
      }
    },
    {
      "elements": [
        {
          "startIndex": 1530,
          "endIndex": 1531,
          "textRun": {
            "content": "\n",
            "textStyle": {}
          }
        }
      ],
      "paragraphStyle": {
        "namedStyleType": "NORMAL_TEXT",
        "direction": "LEFT_TO_RIGHT"
      }
    },
    {
